                    round2_brief:         `<p>Decompose the bias. Three sources map to three methodological responses:</p><p>• <strong>Time-invariant firm-level confounders</strong> (founder quality, location, culture) → absorbed by firm fixed effects.<br>• <strong>Common time shocks</strong> (2020 pandemic, 2022 commodity spike) → absorbed by year fixed effects.<br>• <strong>Selection on time-varying trends</strong> (already-accelerating firms applied) → not addressed by FE; needs IV (1321), DiD (1322) or RD (1323).</p><p>Total Var(y) = Between-firm + Within-firm. The between scatter shows β ≈ 0.18 (selection-dominated). The within scatter shows β ≈ 0.04. The methodological move is to identify β from within-firm variation only.</p><em>Pick a specification that targets the dominant bias source.</em>`,

                    round3_title:         `Round 3 — Apply the method`,
                    round3_brief:         `<p>Apply the within transformation explicitly: (y_it − ȳ_i) = β · (training_it − training̅_i) + (ε_it − ε̅_i). Time-invariant firm quality drops out. Equivalently, run least-squares dummy variables (LSDV) with one dummy per firm.</p><p>Random effects is more efficient if α_i is uncorrelated with training_it — but Round 1 diagnostics already showed massive correlation. The <strong>Hausman (1978) test</strong> compares FE and RE: if it rejects (and here H = 38.6, p < 0.001), RE is inconsistent and you must report FE.</p><p>The dashboard returns: β̂_FE = +5.7%, β̂_TWFE = +4.2%, β̂_RE = +7.8%. The 12% has shrunk by more than half.</p><em>Pick the estimator and run the Hausman test as your diagnostic. The honest answer is whichever survives the test.</em>`,

                    round4_title:         `Round 4 — Check robustness`,
                    round4_brief:         `<p>The Chief Economist returned your draft with three flags:</p><p>• <strong>Cluster-robust SEs at the firm level</strong> (Bertrand-Duflo-Mullainathan 2004): conventional SEs in serially-correlated panels are typically too tight by 2–5×. With clustering, β̂_TWFE = +4.2%, SE = 0.038, t = 1.1, p = 0.27.<br>• <strong>Parallel-trends event study</strong>: γ_{−1} = 0.036, marginally significant. Treated firms were already pulling ahead pre-treatment.<br>• <strong>Heterogeneity</strong>: small manufacturing β ≈ +9.4%, large services β ≈ −0.8%. The pooled average buries real structure.</p><em>Pick the SE treatment, run a final diagnostic, and frame your memo. The credibility-revolution framing — best estimate under stated assumption — beats true causal effect.</em>`,

                    fb_run_summary:       `<strong>Specification result:</strong> β̂ = {beta}, SE = {se}, t = {t}, p = {p}.`,
                    fb_round1_pooled:     `Reasonable starting point: pooled OLS is the baseline you compare everything to. The 12.0% is precise (SE = 0.018) but it is not yet credible — Plot A and Plot B already show selection. Use this as your null specification, not your final one.`,
                    fb_round1_fe_one:     `Aggressive but defensible. Firm FE absorbs founder quality, location, culture. Estimate halves to ~5.7%. Move forward, then ask whether year-specific shocks remain.`,
                    fb_round1_fe_two:     `You skipped the build-up. The +4.2% is the right destination, but a defensible memo shows the audience the journey from 12.0% to 4.2%. Keep this for Round 3 and re-run pooled OLS as the baseline first.`,
                    fb_round2_pooled:     `Plot A says your treated firms were already +8.3% more productive in 2018. The 12.0% is comparing apples to better-managed apples. Re-run with firm FE.`,
